{
  "image_name": "0d73657fad15262ae8e9a595432c4759.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how plasmid transfer affects bacterial metabolism and fitness. Overall, it links conjugation efficiency, the immediate cost of acquiring plasmids, and the longer-term fitness burden carried by recipient cells.\n\nAt the top, higher metabolic activity is associated with greater carbon consumption and appears to promote conjugation, with signaling pathways such as cAMP/protein kinase A and quorum sensing highlighted as factors that raise transfer efficiency. This suggests that active growth or signaling-rich conditions can make plasmid exchange more likely.\n\nThe middle section focuses on acquisition cost. Right after conjugation, the recipient cell seems to experience stress, including activation of the SOS response and disruption of plasmid regulatory networks. Nutrient conditions and growth state also appear to influence how costly plasmid acquisition is, with newly plasmid-bearing cells showing a higher burden under some conditions.\n\nThe bottom section illustrates fitness cost in the absence of selection. Cells carrying new plasmids show a growth burden compared with plasmid-free cells that maintain high growth. The burden is tied to altered metabolism and gene expression, including upregulation of multidrug efflux genes, downregulation of biofilm genes, reduced amino acid synthesis and transport, and the energetic cost of making plasmid-encoded RNA and protein.",
